    "turn_text": "two years, first year, et cetera. So the answer is absolutely yes, but you've got to have a strategy. And here's my two-part strategy. One part is what we're doing right now in the primaries. We talk, I talk, about the things I care about and what they look like. Try to get them out there, try to get them out there enough to tell that they can be vetted. So that, not just you win on them, but you've built the political movement behind them. So you're not starting out on day one. in a new administration turning around saying, okay, I want to explain to everybody what universal child care is. No, no, no, it's that people have been talking about it for a year by that point. And they say, I'm all in, I'm there. That helps keep both our party in the right position and gives us a great place to push back against the opponents. So that's part of it, the work we're doing right now. But the other part of it, in my view, we got to attack head on the corruption in Washington. Think of all the things that we want as a country that we want to see seriously, universal childcare, reduce the student loan debt burden, raise the minimum wage, protect healthcare for every single American. These core economic pieces plus how much support there is now for a Green New Deal, how much support there is for better gun safety. Why don't this things happen? Because Washington is working for the American people. Washington's work of this really thin slice at the top. So I think the place we start is we got to come in and punch right back at the influence of money and politics. And the way to do that, I've got this giant anti-corruption bill, biggest-sense Watergate. It's a giant bill because we have a giant problem. But it does things like inslobbing, as we know it, blocks the revolving door between Wall Street and Main Street, tells Congressman they're not going to be able to get jobs. out there in the industry, lobbying for it after they leave, the things that change the",
