    "turn_text": "politicians are more responsive to their donors than their voters. But did you watch that during the tax? When the Republicans were passing their tax bill, a couple of them just came out and said it. Why were they passing a tax giveaway to millionaires and billionaires? Because they were hearing from their donors who said, if you don't get that thing passed, don't come back and ask me for more money. They literally said it out loud. It was amazing. So now the Republican Party is easy to beat up on that because their whole point of existence seems to be to give money to the billionaires and multinational corporations and their donors, whoever their donors happen to be. But let's be honest about the Democratic Party. That also definitely happens within the Democratic Party. I think less so than the Republican Party, but unfortunately it's far too pervasive. Now, but the problem is one of the issues is in Washington, There's this idea about you gotta get along with other Democrats and you're all on the same team and don't challenge your colleagues, etc. Now you're in a primary where by definition, you guys are running against each other and that's life and that's politics and that's",
